{
  "dom_id": "step-q54418",
  "name": "Question 7",
  "problem": "$\\operatorname{sec}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})=$",
  "worked_solution": "First, we convert $\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ radians to an equivalent measure in degrees. This gives\n\n$$\n(\\frac{\\pi }{4})(\\frac{{180}^{∘}}{\\pi })=\\frac{{180}^{∘}}{4}={45}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})=\\operatorname{cos}⁡{45}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nFinally, since we know that $\\operatorname{cos}⁡{45}^{∘}=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sec}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡{45}^{∘}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
